507
00:37:27,600 --> 00:37:33,500
So what we have here is we
have a sequence of functions

508
00:37:33,500 --> 00:37:37,780
and f defined on
some interval into R.

509
00:37:37,780 --> 00:37:40,700
And then we say that
this sequence converges

510
00:37:40,700 --> 00:37:56,460
to f uniformly if, for all
epsilon greater than 0,

511
00:37:56,460 --> 00:38:00,900
there exists a capital N so
that if little n is bigger

512
00:38:00,900 --> 00:38:08,910
than this capital N,
then f of x minus fn of x

513
00:38:08,910 --> 00:38:13,050
is smaller than
epsilon for all x.

514
00:38:16,670 --> 00:38:24,150
And again, the uniformness,
it holds for all x.

515
00:38:24,150 --> 00:38:28,170
You can use the same
capital N for all x.

516
00:38:33,030 --> 00:38:45,550
And so now, now what you have
is that if a sequence converges

517
00:38:45,550 --> 00:38:50,590
uniformly, then it
converges pointwise.

518
00:38:50,590 --> 00:38:54,950
So we have the following
lemma that follows directly

519
00:38:54,950 --> 00:38:58,110
from the definition.

520
00:38:58,110 --> 00:38:58,660
So lemma.

521
00:39:01,510 --> 00:39:16,890
So a uniform
convergence of fn to f

522
00:39:16,890 --> 00:39:37,730
implies pointwise
convergence of fn to f.

523
00:39:37,730 --> 00:39:38,350
OK.

524
00:39:38,350 --> 00:39:43,330
So uniform convergence
implies pointwise convergence.

525
00:39:43,330 --> 00:39:44,910
And that's clear.

526
00:39:44,910 --> 00:39:47,610
That's really just
the definition

527
00:39:47,610 --> 00:39:51,090
if I had written out
what it means for--

528
00:39:51,090 --> 00:39:54,050
so this here, I've
written out what it means

529
00:39:54,050 --> 00:39:57,330
to have uniform convergence.

530
00:39:57,330 --> 00:39:59,530
If it was pointwise
convergence, then

531
00:39:59,530 --> 00:40:01,190
you have these functions again.

532
00:40:01,190 --> 00:40:04,420
And you have that this
converges to that pointwise.

533
00:40:04,420 --> 00:40:07,360
It means that the sequence fn--

534
00:40:07,360 --> 00:40:12,340
so once you fix x, then
the sequence fn of x--

535
00:40:12,340 --> 00:40:23,820
so once x is fixed, then
these here converge to this.

536
00:40:23,820 --> 00:40:26,660
But saying that this
sequence here converge

537
00:40:26,660 --> 00:40:29,380
means that for all
epsilon greater than 0,

538
00:40:29,380 --> 00:40:35,820
there exists an N. But this N
is now allowed to depend on x so

539
00:40:35,820 --> 00:40:39,340
that if little n is bigger
than this capital N,

540
00:40:39,340 --> 00:40:46,380
then fn of x minus f of x
is smaller than epsilon.

541
00:40:46,380 --> 00:40:52,220
So you see that clearly uniform
convergence implies pointwise.

542
00:40:52,220 --> 00:40:55,820
And again, uniform just
means that this capital N

543
00:40:55,820 --> 00:40:58,320
can be chosen to be
the same for all x.

544
00:41:01,780 --> 00:41:04,850
So now let's look
at the examples.

545
00:41:04,850 --> 00:41:09,070
Unfortunately, I erased
the most important one.

546
00:41:09,070 --> 00:41:11,530
So I will just go
through it again.

547
00:41:11,530 --> 00:41:15,670
Let me just talk about
the example again.

548
00:41:15,670 --> 00:41:22,450
And then we'll see why
this example here does not.

549
00:41:22,450 --> 00:41:25,550
So we have this
example from before,

550
00:41:25,550 --> 00:41:32,550
where the sequence of
functions was x to the n.

551
00:41:32,550 --> 00:41:36,270
And x here was between 0 and 1.

552
00:41:36,270 --> 00:41:39,870
And then we had
this function f that

553
00:41:39,870 --> 00:41:43,270
was this function that
was not continuous.

554
00:41:43,270 --> 00:41:47,150
But this function was
given to be equal to 0

555
00:41:47,150 --> 00:41:53,950
if x here is
strictly less than 1

556
00:41:53,950 --> 00:41:59,602
and it was 1 if x is equal to 1.

557
00:41:59,602 --> 00:42:03,116
That was this function f.

558
00:42:03,116 --> 00:42:07,410
And we know-- we've already
shown that the fn converges

559
00:42:07,410 --> 00:42:09,010
to f pointwise.

560
00:42:09,010 --> 00:42:12,150
But we want to see now--

561
00:42:12,150 --> 00:42:21,330
so I claim that fn
does not converge.

562
00:42:21,330 --> 00:42:29,690
So this does not
converge uniformly to f.

563
00:42:29,690 --> 00:42:33,150
We know already that
it converges pointwise.

564
00:42:33,150 --> 00:42:39,050
And I claim that this sequence
does not converge uniformly.

565
00:42:39,050 --> 00:42:42,850
So now, why is this the case?

566
00:42:42,850 --> 00:42:47,850
Well, this is because, you
see, by the intermediate value

567
00:42:47,850 --> 00:42:54,330
theorem, the functions
fn are continuous.

568
00:42:54,330 --> 00:42:57,490
At 0, they take the value 0.

569
00:42:57,490 --> 00:43:00,300
At 1, they take the value 1.

570
00:43:00,300 --> 00:43:04,360
So for each n, by the
intermediate value theorem--

571
00:43:22,220 --> 00:43:40,220
since each fn is continuous,
then there exists an xn,

572
00:43:40,220 --> 00:43:44,140
but it really will depend on n.

573
00:43:44,140 --> 00:43:52,800
So that fn of xn will
take the value one half.

574
00:43:52,800 --> 00:43:55,200
So the functions here--

575
00:43:55,200 --> 00:43:56,820
this is the functions--

576
00:43:56,820 --> 00:43:57,500
oops.

577
00:43:57,500 --> 00:44:00,320
This is the functions.

578
00:44:00,320 --> 00:44:04,760
This is the function fn.

579
00:44:04,760 --> 00:44:07,240
At 0, it takes a value of 0.

580
00:44:07,240 --> 00:44:10,620
At 1, it takes a value 1.

581
00:44:10,620 --> 00:44:16,100
So there is somewhere
where a half is achieved.

582
00:44:16,100 --> 00:44:18,060
And this is at this-- it's here.

583
00:44:21,700 --> 00:44:23,100
So you have that.

584
00:44:23,100 --> 00:44:27,560
And again, this xn
is depending on n.

585
00:44:27,560 --> 00:44:30,800
And the thing is
that as n gets large,

586
00:44:30,800 --> 00:44:36,040
this point will have to
move very close to 1.

587
00:44:36,040 --> 00:44:44,640
But if you're now looking
at fn of x minus f of x--

588
00:44:51,440 --> 00:44:52,800
so we want to--

589
00:44:52,800 --> 00:44:55,240
if it was converging
uniformly, it

590
00:44:55,240 --> 00:44:58,770
would mean that this difference
here would be smaller

591
00:44:58,770 --> 00:45:01,810
than some epsilon for all x.

592
00:45:01,810 --> 00:45:09,170
But if you're just looking
at this point here,

593
00:45:09,170 --> 00:45:12,210
this difference
here is this point

594
00:45:12,210 --> 00:45:16,610
here, xn, will lie strictly--

595
00:45:16,610 --> 00:45:22,210
xn here will lie-- this is
where this value here is a half.

596
00:45:22,210 --> 00:45:23,590
It's not 1.

597
00:45:23,590 --> 00:45:28,770
So it's strictly
between 0 and 1.

598
00:45:28,770 --> 00:45:37,210
So you see that for each
n, this function f at xn,

599
00:45:37,210 --> 00:45:38,950
this will always be 0.

600
00:45:38,950 --> 00:45:43,110
This is because the
function f is 0 except at 1.

601
00:45:43,110 --> 00:45:45,850
And this point is
definitely not 1.

602
00:45:45,850 --> 00:45:47,450
So you have that.

603
00:45:47,450 --> 00:45:53,130
So you see that this thing
here, this here is 0.

604
00:45:53,130 --> 00:45:56,560
So this is just the value
of this, the absolute value.

605
00:45:56,560 --> 00:45:57,720
But that's actually a half.

606
00:45:57,720 --> 00:46:01,580
So this is always a half.

607
00:46:01,580 --> 00:46:06,020
So the function would converge
uniformly as long as--

608
00:46:09,260 --> 00:46:13,700
so uniform convergence
would mean--

609
00:46:13,700 --> 00:46:15,560
so uniform convergence.

610
00:46:21,140 --> 00:46:29,540
So now let's show.

611
00:46:29,540 --> 00:46:40,240
So let's prove that fn does
not converge uniformly to f.

612
00:46:45,300 --> 00:46:51,500
And so I just pick my epsilon.

613
00:46:51,500 --> 00:46:57,810
So I pick an epsilon
less than half.

614
00:46:57,810 --> 00:47:03,270
And then if it was
converging-- and suppose not--

615
00:47:08,000 --> 00:47:17,700
that is, suppose fn
converges to f uniformly.

616
00:47:21,440 --> 00:47:23,180
This means that for all epsilon.

617
00:47:23,180 --> 00:47:25,140
But I just use
this epsilon here.

618
00:47:25,140 --> 00:47:29,040
I just use this one, epsilon.

619
00:47:29,040 --> 00:47:37,440
But then I would have that
there exists capital N so that--

620
00:47:37,440 --> 00:47:42,040
I mean, I can even take
it to be equal to a half.

621
00:47:42,040 --> 00:47:47,560
So there exists
capital N such that

622
00:47:47,560 --> 00:47:53,930
if little n is bigger than
capital N, then this thing here,

623
00:47:53,930 --> 00:48:01,850
minus f of x, would have to
be strictly less than half

624
00:48:01,850 --> 00:48:02,910
for all x.

625
00:48:06,730 --> 00:48:09,970
If it was converging
uniformly, then in particular,

626
00:48:09,970 --> 00:48:15,970
for this one choice of epsilon,
you would have this for all x.

627
00:48:15,970 --> 00:48:19,310
But we already know that
if we are looking at--

628
00:48:19,310 --> 00:48:22,530
now depending on what
this is, but that's fine.

629
00:48:22,530 --> 00:48:26,450
If you insert x equal
to xn, then this

630
00:48:26,450 --> 00:48:28,990
is actually equal to a half.

631
00:48:28,990 --> 00:48:31,410
So this here does not hold.

632
00:48:31,410 --> 00:48:32,830
This is the contradiction.

633
00:48:39,090 --> 00:48:42,680
Now, in the other example
that we looked at before--

634
00:48:50,810 --> 00:48:54,840
in the other example
we have that--

635
00:48:58,830 --> 00:49:03,470
in the other example,
which is this example here,

636
00:49:03,470 --> 00:49:06,410
this sequence here
is converging.

637
00:49:06,410 --> 00:49:10,590
And I'll return to
this in a minute.

638
00:49:10,590 --> 00:49:14,470
So in this case,
these here converge

639
00:49:14,470 --> 00:49:26,350
to f uniformly on
each interval--

640
00:49:26,350 --> 00:49:27,850
on each bounded interval.

641
00:49:27,850 --> 00:49:35,550
So each interval
of the form minus L

642
00:49:35,550 --> 00:49:38,870
to L. So on each
bounded interval.

643
00:49:38,870 --> 00:49:39,670
OK.

644
00:49:39,670 --> 00:49:42,540
And we will talk about
this more in just a second.

645
00:49:45,910 --> 00:49:48,820
OK.

646
00:49:48,820 --> 00:49:58,200
And because this is
the content of what's

647
00:49:58,200 --> 00:50:01,480
called Weierstrass M-test.

648
00:50:01,480 --> 00:50:06,800
So this holds more generally
that if you take a sequence--

649
00:50:06,800 --> 00:50:07,560
so let's see.

650
00:50:07,560 --> 00:50:09,420
Weierstrass M-test.

651
00:50:14,840 --> 00:50:31,600
So in Weierstrass M-test,
you have a sequence

652
00:50:31,600 --> 00:50:36,980
of functions, fn, and you
have that these sequence--

653
00:50:36,980 --> 00:50:45,640
so fn, sorry, is functions
on some interval into R.

654
00:50:45,640 --> 00:50:58,770
And then you have that the fn
are bounded by some constant Mn.

655
00:50:58,770 --> 00:51:02,810
And then you have that the Mn--

656
00:51:02,810 --> 00:51:05,070
so these are clearly
non-negative numbers.

657
00:51:05,070 --> 00:51:08,630
And you have that
this series here--

658
00:51:12,010 --> 00:51:16,190
this here is finite.

659
00:51:16,190 --> 00:51:19,470
So this is a series of
non-negative numbers.

660
00:51:19,470 --> 00:51:23,290
So to say that this
series is summable

661
00:51:23,290 --> 00:51:24,940
is the same as saying
that it's finite.

662
00:51:29,330 --> 00:51:33,130
And now the claim is-- so
then Weierstrass M-test is--

663
00:51:38,130 --> 00:51:41,910
so this is that if
this here is the case,

664
00:51:41,910 --> 00:51:49,570
then this sequence converges
to some f uniformly.

665
00:51:53,870 --> 00:51:56,480
That's Weierstrass M-test.

666
00:51:59,790 --> 00:52:00,990
Yeah?

667
00:52:00,990 --> 00:52:02,510
AUDIENCE: What does
this mean here?

668
00:52:02,510 --> 00:52:05,168
Is it the absolute
value of the function?

669
00:52:05,168 --> 00:52:06,210
TOBIAS COLDING: Oh, yeah.

670
00:52:06,210 --> 00:52:09,870
So this here-- so
this here means--

671
00:52:09,870 --> 00:52:15,350
this here just means
that fn of x is less than

672
00:52:15,350 --> 00:52:17,730
or equal to M for all x.

673
00:52:25,070 --> 00:52:26,030
Yeah.

674
00:52:26,030 --> 00:52:31,370
That's a kind of quite
common used situation.

675
00:52:34,030 --> 00:52:35,918
OK.

676
00:52:35,918 --> 00:52:37,620
So let's try to prove that.

677
00:52:45,270 --> 00:52:47,410
So we are looking at--

678
00:52:53,000 --> 00:52:54,900
so suppose we look at--

679
00:52:54,900 --> 00:52:58,080
suppose we define Sn of x.

680
00:52:58,080 --> 00:53:03,880
So this is just when you're
summing from k equal to 0

